WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.682
Choose local models by hardware fit, task fit, memory, speed, quality, stability, privacy, and operational needs.

00:00:08.682 --> 00:00:14.388
No parameter count, benchmark, or model family is universally best.

00:00:14.388 --> 00:00:22.078
Inventory CPU, GPU, VRAM, unified memory, system RAM, storage, acceleration support, power, and cooling.

00:00:22.078 --> 00:00:28.279
Evaluate the complete model, quantization, runtime, context, offload, and concurrency configuration.

00:00:28.279 --> 00:00:34.481
Smaller models can be faster, stable, and excellent for focused work.

00:00:34.481 --> 00:00:42.171
Larger models may improve difficult tasks but increase memory, latency, power, and deployment complexity.

00:00:42.171 --> 00:00:45.892
Quantization and context reshape those tradeoffs.

00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:56.559
Benchmark representative prompts repeatedly and record quality, refusals, hallucinations, first-token delay, prompt speed, generation, memory, thermals, failures, and workflow success.

00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:03.753
Start with a smaller reliable model, then scale when measured shortcomings justify it.

00:01:03.753 --> 00:01:15.908
The best local model is the one that performs acceptably on the actual machine for the actual tasks with enough margin to operate.
